  "text": "A black hole is an astronomical phenomenon characterized by the presence of a gravitational field that traps objects like stars, gas, and dust. This trapping causes the objects to be pulled into a point of infinite gravity, known as the event horizon. Beyond this point, no light, including visible and ultraviolet light, can escape the black hole's gravitational pull, which means that all matter cannot be observed from outside the event horizon.\n\nThe concept of a black hole was first proposed by American astronomer S. J. White in 1965. It was later confirmed by other astronomers and physicists, including John Archibald Wheeler and Albert Einstein. Black holes are fascinating and have been the subject of much study and research over the years.\n\nWhile light cannot escape a black hole, there are other phenomena that can be observed from beyond its gravitational field, such as the emission of radiation and the formation of black hole-like objects through binary star systems.\n\n[ Prompt: 1428.7 t/s | Generation: 74.3 t/s ]\n\n\nExiting...\n",
